      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Taft's first post office opened in the 19th century and remained in service until 1967, when mail was redirected to Hahnville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taft%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1909 completion of a railroad line linked Taft to regional trade, but the 1918 shutdown of the Cummings‑Moberly Cypress Company began the town's decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/taft-louisiana-ghost-town/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Historic structures such as the Zeringue House and Holy Rosary Cemetery still stand, offering a glimpse into Taft's pre‑industrial era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/taft-louisiana-ghost-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Taft was part of the German Coast settlement, where German immigrants farmed lands that supplied New Orleans markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/taft-louisiana-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A thriving cypress mill in Taft attracted workers until its 1918 closure, marking the end of the town's industrial boom.",
